{"cuisine/tlingit-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/tlingit-cuisine","n":"Tlingit cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Southeast Alaska","names":"Lingít","intro":[["The Tlingit live along Southeast Alaska's coast and islands, where food comes from both the forest and the sea. ",["Salmon","ingredient/salmon"]," is the center of the year, smoked and dried for winter. In spring, ",["Herring","ingredient/herring"]," eggs are gathered on hemlock branches or kelp, and ",["Eulachon","ingredient/eulachon"]," are rendered into a rich oil."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Alaska Native Heritage Center","url":"https://www.alaskanative.net/"},{"name":"University of Alaska Museum of the North, Alaska Native cultures guide","url":"https://uaf.edu/museum/education/docents/pdfs/NC2-3_Intro_2014.pdf"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"tlingit-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tlingit_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/cuisine-of-the-pennsylvania-dutch":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-the-pennsylvania-dutch","n":"Cuisine of the Pennsylvania Dutch","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine of the Mid-Atlantic United States","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-mid-atlantic-united-states"],["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["Pennsylvania Dutch cuisine is the typical and traditional fare of the Pennsylvania Dutch."],["Pennsylvania Dutch cuisine reflects influences of the Pennsylvania Dutch's German heritage, agrarian society, and rejection of rapid change."],["It is common to find Pennsylvania Dutch cuisine throughout the Philadelphia, Allentown, and Lancaster regions of Pennsylvania."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-the-pennsylvania-dutch"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_the_Pennsylvania_Dutch"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Cuisine of the Mid-Atlantic United States","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-mid-atlantic-united-states"],["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Pennsylvania Dutch Country Cooking","ref":"cookbook/pennsylvania-dutch-country-cooking","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"William Woys Weaver","note":"Traditional Pennsylvania Dutch recipes from a food historian of the region.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781558595682","suggested":true},{"name":"As American as Shoofly Pie: The Foodlore and Fakelore of Pennsylvania Dutch Cuisine","ref":"cookbook/as-american-as-shoofly-pie-the-foodlore-and-fakelore-of-pennsylvania-dutch-cuisine","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"William Woys Weaver","note":"Food history of Pennsylvania Dutch cooking with recipes, published by the University of Pennsylvania Press.","url":"https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.9783/9780812207712/html","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Fasnacht (doughnut)","dish/fasnacht-doughnut"],["Lebanon bologna","dish/lebanon-bologna"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Cuisine of the Mid-Atlantic United States","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-mid-atlantic-united-states"],["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/north-karnataka":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/north-karnataka","n":"Uttara Karnataka cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Karnataka cuisine","cuisine/karnataka-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["North Karnataka,  is a geographical region in Deccan Plateau from 300 to 730 metres (980 to 2,400 ft) elevation that constitutes the north region of the Karnataka state in India and the region consists of 14 districts. It is drained by the Krishna River and its tributaries the Bhima, Ghataprabha, Malaprabha, and Tungabhadra. North Karnataka lies within the Deccan thorn scrub forests ecoregion, which extends north into eastern Maharashtra."],["North Karnataka consists of total 14 districts and comprises the regions known as (Kalyan-Karnataka) – Kalaburagi division  and (Kittur-Karnataka) – Belagavi division. It includes districts of Bagalkote, Bijapur, Gadag, Dharwad, Haveri, Uttara kannada, Belagavi, Bellary, Bidar, Kalaburagi, Koppal, Raichur, Vijayanagara, Yadgir."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"north-karnataka"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Karnataka"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Karnataka cuisine","cuisine/karnataka-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Karnataka cuisine","cuisine/karnataka-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"ingredient/rapini":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/rapini","n":"Rapini","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Rapini is a green cruciferous vegetable, with the leaves, buds, and stems all being edible; the buds somewhat resemble broccoli. Rapini is known for its bitter taste, and is particularly associated with Mediterranean cuisine. It is a particularly rich dietary source of vitamin K."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-rapini"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rapini"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Galician cuisine","cuisine/galician-cuisine",[["Caldo galego","dish/caldo-galego"]]],["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine",[["Bacalhau à minhota","dish/bacalhau-a-minhota"]]],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cui